{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Yes, as a statement of the Manual’s approach to capacity to distinguish: the scent should be separate from the goods, neither natural nor expected, and added to identify the applicant’s goods.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~2",
          "quote": "To be capable of distinguishing the applicant's goods, the scent needs to be something apart from the goods themselves. It should be neither a natural characteristic nor an expected characteristic of the product, but something added to identify the applicant's goods from those of others in the same market."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual treats a product’s natural scent as having no inherent adaptation to distinguish, because it refers to the goods rather than their trade source.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1",
          "quote": "The natural scent of a product will have no inherent adaptation to distinguish the goods."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1",
          "quote": "These scents either form the goods themselves or are a natural attribute of the goods. The scent thus refers to the goods, and not to the trade source."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Merely adding a scent is not enough under the Manual: masking scents have a functional purpose, and fragrances common to the trade are also treated as not capable of distinguishing.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1",
          "quote": "A masking scent has a functional purpose, and is not capable of distinguishing for that reason."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1",
          "quote": "While it cannot be said that a lemon scent is \"descriptive\" of such goods as there is no specific relationship between lemon and the cleaning qualities of such goods, a lemon scent would be treated as not being capable of distinguishing because it is common to the trade."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual also requires a graphical representation and a precise, accurate description identifying the scent and how it is to be used for the claimed goods or services.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/1",
          "quote": "The application must include a graphical representation of the scent mark."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part21/7/7/1",
          "quote": "The applicant must include a precise and accurate description of the scent that will be entered as an endorsement of the application. The description must include both what the scent is, and how it is to be used in respect of the goods or services claimed."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The statutory acceptance rule is that, after examination, the Registrar must accept unless satisfied that the application does not comply with the Act or that a ground for rejection exists under the Act.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s33",
          "quote": "(1) The Registrar must, after the examination, accept the application unless he or she is satisfied that: (a) the application has not been made in accordance with this Act; or (b) there are grounds under this Act for rejecting it."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied passages therefore support the Manual’s distinction between added, source-identifying scents and natural smells, but do not establish an absolute statutory rule that a natural scent can never be accepted; the supplied legislative coverage is insufficient to resolve that broader proposition.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
